The function dereferences two raw pointers, `time1` and `time0`, to read `tv_sec` and `tv_nsec`. Crown reports both as single (`Ptr`), immutable (`Imm`) and transient (`Transient`). Each is a borrowed pointer to one `timespec` that is only read.

- A single immutable transient pointer maps to a shared reference, `&T`.
- Without libc, `libc::timespec` is no longer available. The idiomatic version could take a struct, but the simplest option with no external types is to pass the fields as scalars. A tuple `(i64, i64)` of `(tv_sec, tv_nsec)` would also work.
- I take `(sec, nsec)` pairs as plain `i64` values. Both fields are `i64` on 64-bit Linux (`time_t` and `c_long`).
- With the pointers gone, no `unsafe` is needed. The `wrapping_sub` calls are kept to preserve the original overflow behavior.

Two pointer parameters become four scalar parameters, so the spec maps each pointed-to struct to its fields. The struct has no standalone idiomatic type here, so I describe the field-level mapping with `ref` pointers on the unidiomatic side.

