2026-11-16 13:39:02 | RESPONSE | sactor.llm:160 | Each `unsafe` block in the original maps to safe Rust as follows:

1. **`fprintf(stderr, ...)` and `exit`**: replaced with `eprintln!` and `std::process::exit(1)`, which is safe.
2. **`std::mem::zeroed()` for `csv_parser`**: this struct holds a `Vec` and `Option<fn>`, so zeroing it would be unsound. `csv_init` initializes the parser, so I use `csv_parser::default()`-style construction by hand. The struct only derives `Clone` and `Debug`, so I construct it explicitly with neutral values and let `csv_init` set it up.
3. **`CString::new`, `fopen`, `strerror(errno)`**: replaced with `std::fs::File::open`. The `io::Error` is printed directly, so no `errno` or `strerror` is needed. A file name with an interior NUL is no longer a special case, because `File::open` simply fails for it.
4. **`fread` into a buffer**: replaced with `Read::read` on the `File`. `fread` returns 0 on error, so an `Err` also ends the loop.
5. **`csv_parse` with raw pointer and length**: now takes `&buf[..n]`. The callbacks are `None` and the user-data pointer is gone.
6. **`clock_gettime` and `timespec`**: replaced with `std::time::Instant`. `elapsed().as_secs_f64()` gives the duration directly, so `diff_timespec` is not needed.
7. **`printf`**: replaced with `println!`. `%f` becomes `{:.6}`.
8. **`fclose`**: the file is closed automatically when `fp` is dropped at the end of the loop iteration.

The original calls `csv_fini` after every file, including after a parse error, and I keep that.

----FUNCTION----
```rust
pub fn main() -> () {
    use std::io::Read;

    let args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();
    let argc = args.len();
    if argc < 2 {
        eprintln!("Usage: csvvalid files");
        std::process::exit(1);
    }

    let mut p = csv_parser {
        pstate: 0,
        quoted: 0,
        spaces: 0,
        entry_buf: Vec::new(),
        entry_pos: 0,
        status: 0,
        options: 0,
        quote_char: 0,
        delim_char: 0,
        is_space: None,
        is_term: None,
        blk_size: 0,
    };
    if csv_init(&mut p, 1) != 0 {
        eprintln!("Failed to initialize csv parser");
        std::process::exit(1);
    }

    let mut buf = [0u8; 1024];
    for name in args.iter().skip(1) {
        let mut pos: usize = 0;
        let mut fp = match std::fs::File::open(name) {
            Ok(f) => f,
            Err(e) => {
                eprintln!("Failed to open {}: {}, skipping", name, e);
                continue;
            }
        };

        let start_time = std::time::Instant::now();
        'main: {
            loop {
                let bytes_read = match fp.read(&mut buf) {
                    Ok(0) => break,
                    Ok(n) => n,
                    Err(_) => break,
                };
                let retval = csv_parse(&mut p, &buf[..bytes_read], None, None);
                if retval != bytes_read {
                    if csv_error(&p) == 1 {
                        println!("{}: malformed at byte {}", name, pos + retval + 1);
                        break 'main;
                    } else {
                        println!(
                            "Error while processing {}: {}",
                            name,
                            csv_strerror(csv_error(&p))
                        );
                        break 'main;
                    }
                }
                pos += bytes_read;
            }
            let time_elapsed = start_time.elapsed().as_secs_f64();
            println!("Time taken to parse csv {}: {:.6} seconds", name, time_elapsed);
            println!("{} well-formed", name);
        }

        drop(fp);
        let _ = csv_fini(&mut p, None, None);
    }
    csv_free(&mut p);
}
```
